ena  that were present in the first fraction  of a second after the big bang namely  what's known as a quark gluon plasma or  qgp for short  and this is an almost simulacrum of what  the universe was like in its earliest  moments when quarks and gluons coalesce  to form the protons and neutrons that  make up ordinary matter that we see in  the universe and that we're made up of  results from the so-called off-center  collisions at rick revealed an  asymmetric separation of the charged  particles that emerged from the  collisions  in each event these charged particles  that were emerging went along the  magnetic field lines generated by the  swirling mass of these colliding  plasmatic ions when the negatively  charged particles came out in the  opposite directions they viewed that as  a hint  pointing to perhaps a broken symmetry so  by probing these broken symmetries  physicists believe they could offer  clues into some of the biggest mysteries  in all of science such as what prevailed  in the most early moments in our  universe if the big bang produced an  excess of matter over antimatter it  would make sense but we have no way to  understand how did that fundamental  asymmetry get there and we actually know  the asymmetry is minute  at the one part per billion level in  other words for every billion protons  there's one billion minus one  antiprotons in the early universe  leaving us with two billion photons from  the annihilation and just one proton one  particle of normal matter left over  so nuclear physicists believe that the  separation asymmetry could be caused by  a chiral property of the magnetic field  leading to an excess of left or right  handedness of the emitted particle  depending on whether or not that  particle is spinning clockwise or  counterclockwise relative to its  direction of motion  and as they believe that this could take  place in the quark gluon plasma and if  this took place in the quark gluon  plasma at rick there's a notion that you  